  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="header-inner">
      <div class="brand">
        <h1>The Wire Wranglers</h1>
        <p class="tagline">Untangling engineering problems—one wire, one line of code, and one very confident AI suggestion at a time.</p>
        <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
          <a href="#team">Meet the team</a>
          <a href="#future-of-se">The future of software engineering</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <div class="header-actions">
        <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
          <span aria-hidden="true">☾</span>
          <span class="toggle-label">Night mode</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
      <h2 id="team-heading">Meet the team</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">One engineer, plenty of systems to understand, and a healthy respect for both hardware gremlins and software that sounds a little too sure of itself.</p>

      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img src="{{image:sarpesh.jpg}}" alt="Photo of Sarpesh">
          <h3>Sarpesh</h3>
          <p class="study">M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering, 3rd semester — Paderborn University</p>
          <p><strong>Interests:</strong> Embedded systems and power electronics, automation and control systems, and exploring how AI tools can speed up engineering work.</p>
          <p><strong>Fun fact:</strong> I spend more time debugging wiring than code, and the wiring usually wins.</p>
          <p><strong>AI in software engineering today:</strong> AI coding agents are great for boilerplate, quick scripts, and explaining unfamiliar code. They still make confident mistakes, so every change needs review and testing. For now, they’re a strong assistant—not a replacement for understanding the system.</p>
          <p><strong>In 5–10 years:</strong> Engineers will spend less time typing and more time specifying, reviewing, and verifying what agents produce. Domain knowledge—especially in electrical and embedded systems—will matter even more when correctness and safety are on the line.</p>
          <p class="hot-take"><strong>Hot take:</strong> An AI that writes code you can't verify isn't saving time; it's just moving the bug to production faster.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p><strong>Our shared view:</strong> AI is already useful for speeding up routine coding, quick scripts, and getting oriented in unfamiliar code. But generated output can be confidently wrong, so human review, testing, and real understanding of the system remain essential.</p>
        <p>Over the next 5–10 years, the job is likely to shift from typing every line toward describing what a system must do, then reviewing and verifying agent-generated work. The more physical, complex, or safety-critical the domain, the more valuable engineering expertise will be. In short: let the agent type; keep a human on the wiring—and the test bench.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <div class="footer-inner">Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · The Wire Wranglers</div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const root = document.documentElement;
      const toggle = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");
      const icon = toggle.querySelector("[aria-hidden='true']");
      const label = toggle.querySelector(".toggle-label");

      function updateToggle(theme) {
        const nextTheme = theme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        toggle.setAttribute("aria-label", `Switch to ${nextTheme} mode`);
        icon.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "☀" : "☾";
        label.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "Day mode" : "Night mode";
      }

      updateToggle(root.getAttribute("data-theme"));

      toggle.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const nextTheme = root.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        root.setAttribute("data-theme", nextTheme);
        updateToggle(nextTheme);
        try {
          localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", nextTheme);
        } catch (_) {
          // Theme still changes for this visit if storage is unavailable.
        }
      });
    })();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
